y hand.  \n- On the flop, you have **top pair, second-best kicker (Queen)** — but note that any King in your opponent’s hand would give them top pair with a better kicker (King > Queen).  \n- However, you also have **overcards and straight draw potential**: an Ace plays well against hands like Kx, Tx9x, or weak queens.  \n- The board has two diamonds, so a flush draw is possible. But unless your opponent holds two diamonds, they don’t have a flush yet.  \n- You are out of position (since opponent acted after you pre-flop), so betting for value or protection makes sense.  \n- Checking could allow opponents to realize equity cheaply or bluff later.\n\nPromising moves:\n1. **Check (0)**: Allows you to see a free turn card, but gives initiative to your opponent. Risky if they bet aggressively with draws or bluffs.\n2. **Raise by 100 (total bet 300)**: A small continuation bet. Shows strength, protects your hand, and extracts value from weaker pairs or draws. Low risk.\n3. **Larger raise (>100)**: Could be used to build the pot or protect your hand, but might scare off worse hands and get called only by better ones (like Kx).\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Overkill at this stage. Too bloated relative to pot size (~600 including both bets). Commits too many chips without needing to.\n\nBest move: A **small raise (100)** acts as a continuation bet, representing strength after your pre-flop raise. It’s consistent with a wide range of strong hands (AK, AQ, KK, QQ, etc.), so it balances your range. It also discourages gutshot or backdoor draw runners.\n\nYou’re trying to regain chip lead (currently down ~9.3K), so controlled aggression is key.\n\nReason:  \nMaking a continuation bet of 100 maintains pressure, is low-risk, and capitalizes on your perceived range advantage from pre-flop aggression. It denies free cards and can win the pot outright against drawing hands.\n\nAnswer: 100'}
