nds. If they have middle pair or weak kings, they might call small bet.\n- **Raise by 100:** Small continuation bet. Low risk, could extract value from worse two pair or overpairs.\n- **Larger raise (>100):** Risky. Could price out worse hands, but also fold out any bluffs or weaker pairs.\n- **All-in (-2):** Overkill unless behind a monster. Unlikely needed here.\n- **Fold (-1):** Too weak — you have a very strong hand.\n\nReason:  \nYou have **two pair, Queens and Sixes, with Ace kicker** — a strong hand on a relatively dry board. No flush or straight possible. Opponent has shown moderate aggression but hasn't re-raised your latest bet. Likely has top pair or second-two-pair. A small bet can extract value from weaker holdings (like K-Q, Q-J, or K-x). Since minimum raise is 100 and it's affordable, a **small value bet** makes sense.\n\nChecking might allow a worse hand to bluff later, but more likely lets them check back and see showdown cheaply. Better to **take initiative** with a small bet.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
